      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve for the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) in the equation \n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}\\right) = 12,\n\\]\n\nwe start by introducing a substitution. Let \\( y = \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4} \\). Substituting \\( y \\) into the equation, we get\n\n\\[\ny^2 + y = 12.\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\( y \\). To solve for \\( y \\), we rearrange it as:\n\n\\[\ny^2 + y - 12 = 0.\n\\]\n\nNext, we factor the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\ny^2 + y - 12 = (y + 4)(y - 3) = 0.\n\\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\n\\[\ny + 4 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y - 3 = 0,\n\\]\n\n\\[\ny = -4 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = 3.\n\\]\n\nNow we have two cases to consider:\n\n### Case 1: \\( y = -4 \\)\n\nSubstitute \\( y = -4 \\) back into \\( y = \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4} \\):\n\n\\[\n-4 = \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}.\n\\]\n\nMultiply both sides by \\( 3x-4 \\):\n\n\\[\n-4(3x-4) = 4x-16.\n\\]\n\nDistribute \\(-4\\):\n\n\\[\n-12x + 16 = 4x - 16.\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\n-12x - 4x = -16 - 16,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-16x = -32.\n\\]\n\nDivide by \\(-16\\):\n\n\\[\nx = 2.\n\\]\n\n### Case 2: \\( y = 3 \\)\n\nSubstitute \\( y = 3 \\) back into \\( y = \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4} \\):\n\n\\[\n3 = \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}.\n\\]\n\nMultiply both sides by \\( 3x-4 \\):\n\n\\[\n3(3x-4) = 4x-16.\n\\]\n\nDistribute 3:\n\n\\[\n9x - 12 = 4x - 16.\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\n9x - 4x = -16 + 12,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n5x = -4.\n\\]\n\nDivide by 5:\n\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{4}{5}.\n\\]\n\nThe possible values for \\( x \\) are \\( x = 2 \\) and \\( x = -\\frac{4}{5} \\). The greatest possible value of \\( x \\) is \\( 2 \\).\n\nThus, the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
